window.__BASIS_DRILLS__ = {"accounting":{"title":"Accounting","questions":[{"prompt":"What does the \"top line\" refer to?","options":["Net income","Revenue (sales)","Gross profit","Operating expenses"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Revenue sits at the top of the income statement — the \"top line.\" Net income at the very bottom is the \"bottom line.\"","source":"The Income Statement"},{"prompt":"How is gross profit calculated?","options":["Revenue minus every operating and non-operating expense","Revenue minus the cost of goods sold (COGS)","Net income with taxes added straight back on","Operating income after interest has been deducted"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Gross profit = Revenue − COGS. It shows core product profitability before operating costs, interest, and taxes.","source":"The Income Statement"},{"prompt":"What does operating profit (EBIT) represent?","options":["Profit remaining after both interest and tax are paid","Total revenue booked before any costs are deducted","Profit from core operations, before interest and tax","The cash currently sitting in the company's bank"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Operating profit = gross profit − operating expenses. It’s earnings before interest and tax (EBIT), reflecting core operations.","source":"The Income Statement"},{"prompt":"What is earnings per share (EPS)?","options":["Net income divided by the shares outstanding","Total revenue divided by the shares outstanding","The share price divided by the firm's earnings","Total dividends divided by reported net income"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"EPS = net income ÷ shares outstanding — the bottom-line profit attributable to each share.","source":"The Income Statement"},{"prompt":"Net income is the same as the cash a company generated during the period.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"False — net income includes non-cash items (e.g. depreciation) and uses accrual accounting. Cash is tracked on the cash flow statement.","source":"The Income Statement"},{"prompt":"Which equation must always hold on the balance sheet?","options":["Assets = Liabilities + Equity","Assets = Revenue − total Expenses","Equity = Assets + all Liabilities","Liabilities = Assets + Equity"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The fundamental accounting equation: Assets = Liabilities + Equity. Assets are financed by debt or owners’ equity.","source":"The Balance Sheet"},{"prompt":"Which of these is a liability?","options":["Cash","Accounts payable","Inventory","Retained earnings"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Accounts payable is money owed to suppliers — a liability. Cash and inventory are assets; retained earnings is equity.","source":"The Balance Sheet"},{"prompt":"What is equity on the balance sheet?","options":["The company's total revenue booked for the year","The owners' residual claim: assets minus liabilities","Exactly the same thing as the company's cash","The total money the company owes to its lenders"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Equity = assets − liabilities — the owners’ residual stake, including share capital and retained earnings.","source":"The Balance Sheet"},{"prompt":"What are retained earnings?","options":["A liability the company owes back to its shareholders","Cumulative profits kept in the business (part of equity)","The revenue the company has booked this year alone","The outstanding money the company owes its suppliers"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Retained earnings are accumulated profits not paid out as dividends — a component of equity.","source":"The Balance Sheet"},{"prompt":"The balance sheet shows a snapshot at a single point in time.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — unlike the income statement (a period), the balance sheet is a position at one date.","source":"The Balance Sheet"},{"prompt":"What are the three sections of the cash flow statement?","options":["Revenue, total costs, and resulting profit","Operating, investing, and financing activities","Assets, liabilities, and shareholders' equity","Current, long-term, and total classifications"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Cash flow is split into operating (CFO), investing (CFI), and financing (CFF) activities.","source":"The Cash Flow Statement"},{"prompt":"Buying new equipment (capex) appears in which section?","options":["Operating","Financing","Investing","It does not appear"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Capital expenditure is an investing activity — cash used to acquire long-term assets.","source":"The Cash Flow Statement"},{"prompt":"Paying a dividend is classified as which type of cash flow?","options":["Operating","Investing","Financing","Non-cash"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Dividends paid are a financing outflow — cash returned to shareholders.","source":"The Cash Flow Statement"},{"prompt":"A common approximation of free cash flow is…","options":["Net income added on top of total revenue","Operating cash flow minus capital expenditure","Total revenue with COGS subtracted from it","Total assets minus the company's liabilities"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Free cash flow ≈ operating cash flow − capex: cash left after maintaining/growing the asset base.","source":"The Cash Flow Statement"},{"prompt":"Why can a profitable company still run out of cash?","options":["Net income uses accruals and non-cash items, so profit ≠ cash","Reported profit is essentially always a negative figure","Cash and accounting profit are one and the same thing","It is entirely down to the dividends that get paid out"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Accrual accounting and non-cash items mean reported profit can diverge from actual cash — hence the cash flow statement.","source":"The Cash Flow Statement"},{"prompt":"Net income links the income statement to the balance sheet via…","options":["Accounts payable owed to the firm's suppliers","Retained earnings (net income − dividends)","Total revenue booked over the period","Inventory still sitting in the warehouse"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Net income minus dividends adds to retained earnings within equity on the balance sheet.","source":"Connecting the Three Statements"},{"prompt":"The net change in cash on the cash flow statement updates which balance-sheet line?","options":["Equity","Cash","Revenue","COGS"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Ending cash = beginning cash + net change in cash — the cash flow statement updates the balance-sheet cash line.","source":"Connecting the Three Statements"},{"prompt":"Why is depreciation added back in operating cash flow?","options":["It is treated as a genuine cash inflow","A non-cash expense that reduced net income","It directly increases the top-line revenue","It is classified as a financing activity"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Depreciation lowers net income but uses no cash, so it’s added back to reconcile profit to operating cash.","source":"Connecting the Three Statements"},{"prompt":"If accounts receivable rise during the period, operating cash flow is…","options":["Somewhat higher than the reported net income","Lower than net income (cash not yet collected)","Completely unaffected by the timing difference","Exactly equal to the top-line revenue figure"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Rising receivables mean revenue was booked but cash hasn’t arrived, reducing operating cash flow relative to profit.","source":"Connecting the Three Statements"},{"prompt":"The three statements are interconnected, so a change in one affects the others.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — that interlock is why integrated three-statement models are central to financial analysis.","source":"Connecting the Three Statements"},{"prompt":"What does the current ratio measure?","options":["The company's long-term profitability outlook","Short-term liquidity: current assets ÷ current liabilities","The size of the dividend the company pays out","The rate at which company revenue is growing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Current ratio = current assets ÷ current liabilities — a gauge of ability to meet short-term obligations.","source":"Key Financial Ratios"},{"prompt":"Debt-to-equity is a measure of…","options":["Liquidity","Leverage","Efficiency","Growth"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Debt-to-equity (total debt ÷ equity) is a leverage ratio — how reliant the company is on debt financing.","source":"Key Financial Ratios"},{"prompt":"Return on equity (ROE) is calculated as…","options":["Net income ÷ equity","Revenue ÷ assets","EBIT ÷ interest","Current assets ÷ current liabilities"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"ROE = net income ÷ equity — the profit generated on shareholders’ invested capital.","source":"Key Financial Ratios"},{"prompt":"Interest coverage (EBIT ÷ interest) tells you…","options":["How fast the company sells through its inventory","How comfortably operating profit covers interest payments","The company's overall net profit margin percentage","The current level of the company's share price"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Interest coverage shows how many times operating profit covers interest — higher means safer debt servicing.","source":"Key Financial Ratios"},{"prompt":"A ratio is most meaningful when compared to peers, history, or industry norms.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — ratios need context; a healthy level varies widely by industry and business model.","source":"Key Financial Ratios"},{"prompt":"Under IFRS 15, a company signs a 3-year SaaS contract for £120,000 (£40,000/year). The customer pays the full £120,000 upfront. How should the company recognise revenue?","options":["£120,000 in year 1 — cash has been received and the contract is signed","£40,000 per year over 3 years — as the service is delivered (over-time recognition)","£120,000 at contract end — when all performance obligations are complete","£60,000 in year 1 and £60,000 in year 2 — split equally between signing and renewal"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is a classic over-time recognition scenario. The customer receives and consumes the SaaS service continuously — control transfers over time, not at a single point. IFRS 15 requires recognising £40,000/year as the service is delivered. The £120,000 received upfront is recognised as a deferred revenue liability and released to the P&L as the service is provided. Recognising all £120,000 in year 1 would be a revenue recognition error (and a common early-stage company temptation).","source":"Revenue Recognition"},{"prompt":"A software company's revenue grows 25% year-over-year. At the same time, days sales outstanding (DSO) jumps from 45 days to 75 days. What does this pattern suggest?","options":["The company is simply growing in a healthy way — a rising DSO is entirely normal during expansion","Possible channel stuffing — sales are outrunning cash collection, so revenue may not be genuine","The company has improved payment terms for customers, benefiting long-term retention","DSO is unrelated to revenue quality — it only affects working capital, not earnings"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"DSO = (Accounts Receivable / Revenue) × days. Rising DSO means the company is collecting cash more slowly than it is booking revenue. When DSO rises sharply alongside strong revenue growth, it is a major red flag: either customers are not paying (credit quality issue) or revenue is being recognised before it is genuinely earned (channel stuffing, premature recognition). Forensic accounting firms specifically flag DSO divergence as one of the most reliable early indicators of revenue fraud.","source":"Revenue Recognition"},{"prompt":"A construction company uses the percentage-of-completion method on a 4-year, £20m contract. After year 2, it has incurred £7m of costs against an estimated £14m total cost. How much revenue should it have recognised in total by year 2?","options":["£10m","£7m","£14m","£20m"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under percentage-of-completion, progress = costs incurred / estimated total costs = £7m / £14m = 50%. Revenue recognised = 50% × £20m contract = £10m. This method matches revenue to the work actually done rather than deferring everything until completion. If actual costs overrun (say total costs end up £16m), the percentage complete changes and future revenue recognition is adjusted — which is why construction company revenue can be volatile.","source":"Revenue Recognition"},{"prompt":"A company's deferred revenue balance has fallen from £50m to £20m over two years while bookings (new contracts signed) have remained flat. What does this signal?","options":["The company is collecting cash faster — a positive liquidity signal","Revenue recognition has been aggressive — backlog is being depleted without equivalent new bookings to replace it","Customers are cancelling contracts — churn risk is high","The company has shifted from subscription to perpetual licence model, which is immaterial for valuation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Deferred revenue = unrecognised future revenue that has already been billed. A shrinking deferred revenue balance when bookings are flat means the company is recognising revenue faster than it is adding new contracted backlog. This is a leading indicator of future revenue pressure: the \"queue\" of future recognised revenue is shrinking. Analysts model deferred revenue movements carefully for SaaS companies, as it signals whether reported revenue growth is being pulled forward from future periods.","source":"Revenue Recognition"},{"prompt":"A company reports adjusted EBITDA of £50m, adding back: £5m of restructuring charges (4th consecutive year), £8m of stock-based compensation, and £3m of M&A transaction costs (deal completed 18 months ago). Which add-back is most questionable?","options":["SBC — it is always legitimate to exclude non-cash charges","Restructuring charges — four consecutive years suggests they are recurring operating costs, not one-time items","M&A transaction costs — these are always excluded regardless of timing","None of them — adjusted EBITDA already correctly excludes every genuinely non-operating item here"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Restructuring charges added back for the 4th consecutive year are the most suspect. By definition, a recurring cost is not \"one-time.\" Companies that restructure every year are effectively labelling a structural cost of their business as exceptional. An independent analyst would include these costs in normalised earnings. SBC is genuinely debated (non-cash but dilutive). M&A costs 18 months after deal close are harder to justify — those should have been fully expensed at the time.","source":"Quality of Earnings"},{"prompt":"A company's net income is £30m but operating cash flow is £8m. What does this likely indicate?","options":["Excellent underlying performance — high reported net income combined with conservative capital spending","Poor earnings quality — much of net income is accruals not cash, hinting at aggressive accounting","The company has high capital expenditure — normal for capital-intensive businesses","The company has paid large dividends, reducing its cash position"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Cash conversion = Operating CF / Net Income = £8m / £30m = 27% — extremely low. When operating cash flow lags net income significantly, it means a large portion of profits exists on paper (accruals, receivables growth, deferred costs) rather than being collected as cash. This is the clearest signal of potential earnings quality issues. Capex affects investing cash flow, not operating cash flow. Dividends affect financing cash flow. The gap between £30m and £8m should trigger deep investigation into working capital movements and accrual items.","source":"Quality of Earnings"},{"prompt":"A new CEO joins a company and immediately takes a £200m write-down on goodwill, restructures the sales force (£30m charge), and writes off slow-moving inventory (£15m). This pattern is known as:","options":["Cookie jar reserve creation — building provisions for future release","\"Big bath\" accounting — maximising write-offs on arrival to set a clean earnings base going forward","Revenue smoothing — deferring income to future periods","Aggressive accrual recognition — accelerating expenses into the current year unnecessarily"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"\"Big bath\" accounting occurs when a new CEO takes maximum write-downs in their first year — on the logic that the previous management is \"blamed\" for the charges, while the new CEO's subsequent performance starts from a clean, lower cost base. Future earnings look stronger because the charges have already been taken. While some of these write-offs may be genuinely necessary, the motivation can lead to over-provisioning — creating a reserve for future earnings management. Auditors and analysts scrutinise first-year write-offs carefully.","source":"Quality of Earnings"},{"prompt":"Why do academics and analysts argue that stock-based compensation (SBC) should NOT be excluded from adjusted EBITDA for technology companies?","options":["Stock-based comp is really a cash expense, since it drains the company's bank account directly","SBC dilutes existing shareholders — it is a real economic cost of compensating employees, even if no cash changes hands","GAAP prohibits the exclusion of SBC from non-GAAP metrics","SBC increases revenue through employee productivity, netting to zero"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"SBC is non-cash — no money leaves the company's bank account. But it dilutes existing shareholders by issuing new equity to employees. Each share issued to an employee is a share that could otherwise be issued to raise capital, or represents value transferred from existing shareholders to employees. For high-SBC tech companies, the gap between GAAP EPS (which includes SBC expense) and adjusted EPS (which excludes it) can be enormous — 50%+ at companies where SBC represents 20-30% of revenue. Excluding it paints an inflated picture of earnings power.","source":"Quality of Earnings"},{"prompt":"A company has DSO of 60 days, DIO of 45 days, and DPO of 30 days. What is the cash conversion cycle?","options":["75 days","135 days","45 days","15 days"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"CCC = DSO + DIO − DPO = 60 + 45 − 30 = 75 days. This means the company takes 75 days from when it pays for raw materials to when it collects cash from customers. During those 75 days, it is funding the working capital gap from its own cash or borrowings. Reducing DIO by 10 days (selling inventory faster) or increasing DPO by 10 days (paying suppliers later) would each reduce the CCC by 10 days and release working capital cash.","source":"Working Capital Analysis"},{"prompt":"A fast-growing software company has a cash conversion cycle of −45 days. Which of the following best explains this?","options":["The company is paying its suppliers extremely late, badly straining its key supplier relationships","Customers pay annual subscriptions upfront (creating deferred revenue) before the company incurs costs to service them","The company has negative net income, which creates a negative CCC","Inventory is being written off, reducing DIO artificially"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A negative CCC means customers pay before the company has to pay its costs — a structural cash advantage. SaaS and subscription businesses often achieve this: annual contracts paid upfront create deferred revenue (liability), while costs (cloud infrastructure, support) are incurred over the year. The company uses customer cash to fund operations. This is why high-quality subscription businesses are capital-efficient despite heavy growth investment. Negative net income does not affect the CCC formula — CCC is about operational cash timing, not profitability.","source":"Working Capital Analysis"},{"prompt":"A company's EBITDA is £40m. Its cash from operations is only £15m. The difference is entirely explained by a £25m increase in accounts receivable. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["The company has invested heavily in acquiring new customers who will pay later; growth outpaces collection","Revenue is growing strongly and all new sales are being collected — a positive sign","The firm extended payment terms to pull revenue forward, but cash hasn't followed — a quality concern","The accounts receivable increase has no impact on cash conversion"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When EBITDA is strong but operating cash flow is weak due to a large receivables build, the first question is whether those receivables are genuinely collectible. Extended payment terms boost reported revenue now but defer cash collection — when the revenue was booked, the performance obligation may have been satisfied, but the economic cash has not arrived. If a growing company must extend terms to maintain growth, it may indicate demand is softer than revenue figures suggest. Always ask: why are receivables growing faster than revenue?","source":"Working Capital Analysis"},{"prompt":"A retailer's inventory days (DIO) jumps from 45 to 80 days while revenue is flat. What does this signal?","options":["The retailer is managing its inventory far more efficiently, and its stock turnover is clearly improving","The retailer is building inventory ahead of an expected demand surge","Unsold inventory is building up — possible demand weakness, pricing pressure, or obsolescence risk","DIO increases are always associated with revenue growth"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When DIO rises sharply with flat revenue, inventory is accumulating faster than it is being sold. For a retailer, this typically signals one or more of: demand is weaker than expected, the product mix has shifted to slower-moving items, or pricing needs to be cut to clear stock (margin risk). Inventory write-downs often follow — which then hits the income statement. A build from 45 to 80 days is a significant deterioration and would trigger analyst concern. The 2022-23 post-COVID inventory glut at many retailers is a real-world example.","source":"Working Capital Analysis"}]},"valuation":{"title":"Valuation","questions":[{"prompt":"What is the market cap formula?","options":["Net income × P/E","Shares outstanding × share price","Total assets − total liabilities","Revenue × margin"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Market cap = shares outstanding × share price.","source":"Market Capitalisation"},{"prompt":"Roughly, what defines a \"large cap\" company?","options":["Below $1bn","Around $300m–$2bn","Typically above ~$10bn","There are no conventions"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Conventionally: large cap ~>$10bn, mid cap ~$2–10bn, small cap ~$300m–$2bn. Thresholds vary by provider.","source":"Market Capitalisation"},{"prompt":"Why does market cap differ from book value?","options":["They are one and the same figure, identical for every listed company on the market","Book value already includes the live share price","Market cap simply ignores the shares outstanding","Market cap is what investors pay today; book value is accounting equity"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Market cap is a forward-looking market price of equity; book value is historical accounting net assets. They rarely match.","source":"Market Capitalisation"},{"prompt":"What happens when a company buys back its own shares?","options":["Shares outstanding fall, concentrating ownership in fewer shares","Shares outstanding rise, spreading value more thinly","The dividend per share is automatically increased","The company's book value can never change at all"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Buybacks reduce the share count (the opposite of dilution), concentrating ownership and often lifting earnings per share.","source":"Market Capitalisation"},{"prompt":"How does free float differ from market cap?","options":["They are the same measure under a different name","Float is the shares available to trade publicly; market cap values all shares","Float counts only the shares held by company insiders","Market cap deliberately excludes the current live share price from its whole calculation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Free float is the portion of shares freely tradable (excluding locked-up insider/strategic holdings); market cap is based on total shares outstanding.","source":"Market Capitalisation"},{"prompt":"What is the P/E formula?","options":["Annual earnings divided by the current share price","The share price divided by the company's revenue","Share price ÷ earnings per share","The share price divided by book value per share"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"P/E = price per share ÷ earnings per share (EPS).","source":"The P/E Ratio"},{"prompt":"What is the difference between forward and trailing P/E?","options":["Trailing uses past 12-month earnings; forward uses estimated future earnings","Forward P/E uses the past twelve months of earnings","They are identical, just labelled differently by data providers","Trailing P/E is built from forward analyst estimates"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Trailing P/E uses the last 12 months of actual earnings; forward P/E uses forecast earnings, so it reflects growth expectations.","source":"The P/E Ratio"},{"prompt":"Why do many tech companies trade at high P/E ratios?","options":["They currently pay unusually large cash dividends","Investors expect rapid future earnings growth","They carry essentially no earnings risk whatsoever","Their accounting book value per share is very high"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A high P/E typically signals the market expects strong earnings growth, so investors pay more per current £1 of earnings.","source":"The P/E Ratio"},{"prompt":"Which is a real limitation of the P/E ratio?","options":["It perfectly captures a company's true intrinsic value in essentially every situation","It fully accounts for every balance-sheet risk","It can never be compared across different companies","It ignores growth and leverage and breaks down with volatile earnings"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"P/E says nothing about growth rate, debt, or earnings quality, and is misleading when earnings are negative or one-off.","source":"The P/E Ratio"},{"prompt":"What does a negative P/E mean?","options":["The company has negative earnings (a loss)","The company's quoted share price has gone negative","The company has chosen to pay no dividend this year","The stock is effectively a risk-free investment now"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A negative P/E arises when EPS is negative — the company is loss-making — so the ratio is generally not meaningful.","source":"The P/E Ratio"},{"prompt":"Company A has ROE = 20%, Net Margin = 10%, Asset Turnover = 1.0×, Leverage = 2.0×. Company B has ROE = 20%, Net Margin = 5%, Asset Turnover = 2.0×, Leverage = 2.0×. Which company has the higher quality ROE?","options":["Company B, because its higher asset turnover clearly shows superior day-to-day operational efficiency","Company A — higher net margins indicate a stronger competitive position and more sustainable returns","Both equally — ROE is identical so there is no quality difference","Impossible to determine without knowing the industry"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Both achieve 20% ROE (10% × 1.0 × 2.0 = 20% and 5% × 2.0 × 2.0 = 20%) but through different paths. Company A's 10% net margin suggests pricing power and a competitive moat — it earns more from each pound of revenue. Company B's 5% margin means it is a lower-quality business competing on volume. High-margin businesses are more resilient in downturns, have more flexibility to invest, and are less vulnerable to cost inflation. A margin-driven ROE is generally considered higher quality than a turnover-driven one at the same leverage.","source":"DuPont ROE Analysis"},{"prompt":"A company's ROE has improved from 12% to 18% over 3 years. DuPont analysis shows: Net Margin unchanged at 8%, Asset Turnover unchanged at 1.0×, Leverage up from 1.5× to 2.25×. What is the correct interpretation?","options":["The underlying business has genuinely improved — a rising ROE always reflects better real performance","ROE rose purely on higher leverage, not real improvement — more risk without more profitability","The company has become more efficient — leverage is a sign of confidence","The ROE improvement is immaterial because margins did not change"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Using DuPont: 8% × 1.0 × 1.5 = 12% (before), 8% × 1.0 × 2.25 = 18% (after). The entire ROE improvement came from borrowing more. Operating profitability (margin) and efficiency (turnover) were unchanged. Leverage amplifies returns but also amplifies losses and risk. An analyst would note that the ROE improvement is financial engineering, not business improvement — and would flag that the higher leverage increases vulnerability to an earnings decline or rate rise.","source":"DuPont ROE Analysis"},{"prompt":"A company has ROE of 18% and a dividend payout ratio of 40%. What is its sustainable growth rate?","options":["18%","7.2%","10.8%","40%"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"SGR = ROE × (1 − payout ratio) = 18% × (1 − 0.40) = 18% × 0.60 = 10.8%. The company retains 60% of its earnings and generates 18% ROE on equity — so it can grow at 10.8% per year without external financing. If the company is growing faster than 10.8%, it must either borrow more, issue equity, or cut the dividend. This is a useful sanity check when analysts see companies projecting aggressive growth without visible financing plans.","source":"DuPont ROE Analysis"},{"prompt":"Bank A has ROE = 14% and ROA = 0.7%, financed with 20× leverage. Bank B has ROE = 14% and ROA = 7%, financed with 2× leverage. Which bank's ROE is more sustainable?","options":["Bank A, because banks are fundamentally designed to operate safely with very high levels of leverage","Bank B — the same ROE is generated with much stronger underlying asset profitability, making it far more resilient to stress","Equal — both deliver 14% ROE to shareholders","Bank A — regulatory capital requirements make high leverage mandatory for banks"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Both deliver 14% ROE, but through very different structures. Bank A: 0.7% ROA × 20× leverage = 14%. Bank B: 7% ROA × 2× leverage = 14%. Bank A's ROE is entirely leverage-dependent: a 5% fall in asset values on 20× leverage wipes out all equity. Bank B can absorb a 50% asset loss before equity is impaired. High-ROA businesses with modest leverage are fundamentally more resilient. The 2008 crisis destroyed banks that operated on thin ROA/high-leverage models. Bank B's ROE quality is incomparably superior.","source":"DuPont ROE Analysis"},{"prompt":"A company has EBIT of £60m, a 25% tax rate, and invested capital of £400m. What is its ROIC?","options":["15%","11.25%","10%","25%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"NOPAT = EBIT × (1 − tax rate) = £60m × (1 − 0.25) = £60m × 0.75 = £45m. ROIC = NOPAT / Invested Capital = £45m / £400m = 11.25%. Note: we use EBIT (not net income) because ROIC measures returns before financing — interest is not subtracted. The tax adjustment converts operating profit to an after-tax basis so ROIC and WACC are comparable on the same post-tax basis.","source":"ROIC and Economic Profit"},{"prompt":"Company X has ROIC = 20% and WACC = 12%. Company Y has ROIC = 8% and WACC = 12%. Both have £1bn of invested capital. What is the EVA for each?","options":["X: £200m created and Y: £80m created — both genuinely add economic value","X: +£80m, Y: −£40m","X: +£80m, Y: −£40m — X creates economic value; Y destroys it","X: £120m, Y: £96m"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"EVA = (ROIC − WACC) × Invested Capital. Company X: (20% − 12%) × £1bn = 8% × £1bn = +£80m of economic profit. Company Y: (8% − 12%) × £1bn = −4% × £1bn = −£40m of economic loss. Despite Y having positive reported profits (8% ROIC generates positive NOPAT), it is destroying value because its returns fall short of capital costs. Every pound Y reinvests reduces economic value. Y should return capital to shareholders rather than grow.","source":"ROIC and Economic Profit"},{"prompt":"A PE firm acquires a business for £300m. The business had pre-acquisition invested capital of £100m and pre-goodwill ROIC of 22%. After the acquisition, including £200m of goodwill, what is the post-goodwill ROIC if NOPAT is £22m?","options":["22%","7.3%","11%","15%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Post-goodwill invested capital = pre-acquisition capital + goodwill = £100m + £200m = £300m. Post-goodwill ROIC = NOPAT / total invested capital = £22m / £300m = 7.3%. The acquisition price was 3× book value. The underlying business is excellent (22% pre-goodwill ROIC) but the PE firm paid such a high premium that the blended return falls to 7.3% — potentially below WACC. This is the \"winner's curse\" in M&A: a great business acquired at too high a price becomes a poor investment.","source":"ROIC and Economic Profit"},{"prompt":"A company's ROIC has declined from 18% to 11% over five years while its WACC remains 9%. What is the most important strategic implication?","options":["The company should accelerate capital investment to recover ROIC through scale","ROIC remains above WACC so value is still being created — no action required","A falling ROIC nearing WACC signals eroding advantage — investigate the cause and consider returning capital","The company should reduce its WACC by taking on more debt to restore the ROIC/WACC spread"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"At 11%, ROIC is still above WACC (9%) — value is being created, but less efficiently than before. The trend is more alarming than the current level: declining ROIC typically signals intensifying competition, pricing pressure, or capital misallocation. If the trend continues and ROIC falls below WACC, the company becomes a value destroyer. Management must investigate the root cause — is it a structural threat to margins or a temporary issue? If the former, a capital return strategy (buybacks, dividends) may be better than continued reinvestment at declining returns.","source":"ROIC and Economic Profit"},{"prompt":"A company acquires a business for £400m. Net identifiable assets at fair value are £120m (including £60m of separately identified intangibles). How much goodwill is recorded?","options":["£400m","£280m","£340m","£60m"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Goodwill = Purchase Price − Fair Value of Net Identifiable Assets = £400m − £120m = £280m. The £120m includes the separately identified intangibles (technology, customer relationships, brand) already allocated to specific line items. Goodwill is the residual — what the acquirer paid above and beyond everything that could be specifically identified and valued. It represents synergies, assembled workforce, and market position. Separately identified intangibles (£60m) will be amortised; the £280m goodwill will not.","source":"Goodwill, Intangibles & Impairment"},{"prompt":"Under IFRS, how is goodwill treated after it is initially recorded?","options":["It is amortised over a fixed 40-year period on a straight-line basis, like other long-life assets","Amortised over its estimated useful life, typically 10-20 years","Not amortised — tested for impairment at least annually and written down only if recoverable amount falls below carrying value","Written off immediately against retained earnings in the year of acquisition"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under IFRS (IAS 36), goodwill is not amortised — it stays on the balance sheet indefinitely at cost unless impaired. Annually, the goodwill is allocated to CGUs and tested: if the recoverable amount (higher of fair value or value-in-use) falls below the carrying value, an impairment charge is taken. The rational is that goodwill from a successful acquisition may retain its value indefinitely (a strong brand or technology can appreciate). The practical problem: this inflates balance sheets of acquisitive companies and delays recognition of overpayment.","source":"Goodwill, Intangibles & Impairment"},{"prompt":"A company takes a £300m goodwill impairment charge. Which of the following correctly describes the impact?","options":["Net income falls by £300m, cash falls by £300m, and total equity also falls by the full £300m","Net income falls by approximately £300m × (1 − tax rate), equity falls by the same amount, but cash is unaffected","Net income is unaffected — impairments bypass the P&L and go directly to equity","Net income falls £300m, equity falls £300m, cash flow from operations falls £300m"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Goodwill impairments flow through the income statement: they reduce pre-tax profit. After applying the tax rate (note: goodwill impairments are often not tax-deductible, so the effective tax impact may be zero), net income falls by approximately the full £300m. This reduces retained earnings (equity) by the same amount. However, it is a non-cash charge: no money leaves the company. Operating cash flow is unaffected. This is why analysts add back impairment charges to reach \"clean\" earnings — the impairment tells you about the past (overpayment or underperformance) but doesn't directly affect current cash generation.","source":"Goodwill, Intangibles & Impairment"},{"prompt":"A company has total assets of £2bn, of which £1.3bn is goodwill from 12 acquisitions made over the past 8 years. What risk does this balance sheet structure present?","options":["None at all — goodwill is a genuinely productive asset that continues to generate real returns","High concentration risk: 65% of assets are goodwill, so any deterioration could trigger big write-downs","Tax risk — goodwill cannot be deducted for tax purposes, creating a deferred tax liability","Liquidity risk — goodwill cannot be sold to meet short-term obligations"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"When goodwill is 65% of total assets, the balance sheet is almost entirely built on acquisition premiums. If the acquired businesses underperform their acquisition case, impairment tests will fail and large write-downs follow — directly hitting reported earnings. Serial acquirers with heavy goodwill balances are particularly vulnerable in recessions when discount rates rise and cash flow projections fall simultaneously (both reduce recoverable amounts). Analysts track goodwill/total assets as a metric of impairment risk concentration, and impairment risk is typically highlighted in equity research when this ratio exceeds 40-50%.","source":"Goodwill, Intangibles & Impairment"},{"prompt":"A stock with a beta of 1.5 would be expected to:","options":["Return 1.5% per year regardless of the market","Move 50% more than the market in each direction","Have a 50% chance of beating the market","Be 1.5× more diversified than a benchmark"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Beta of 1.5 means the stock is expected to amplify market moves by 50% — up and down. If the market rises 10%, the stock would be expected to rise 15%; if the market falls 10%, the stock would fall 15%.","source":"Beta & Market Risk"},{"prompt":"According to CAPM, investors should only be compensated for:","options":["Unsystematic (company-specific) risk","Systematic (market) risk","Both systematic and unsystematic risk","Volatility that exceeds 15% per year"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CAPM says unsystematic risk can be diversified away for free, so the market only rewards investors for bearing systematic (market) risk — captured by beta.","source":"Beta & Market Risk"},{"prompt":"If you hold 50% in a stock with beta 2.0 and 50% in a stock with beta 0, the portfolio beta is:","options":["0","1.0","1.5","2.0"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Portfolio beta = weighted average of component betas. (0.5 × 2.0) + (0.5 × 0) = 1.0. The portfolio moves in line with the market on average.","source":"Beta & Market Risk"},{"prompt":"Which type of stock typically has a low or negative beta?","options":["High-growth technology companies","Leveraged small-cap companies","Utilities and consumer staples","Emerging market equities"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Utilities and consumer staples sell things people buy regardless of the economic cycle (electricity, food). Their earnings are stable, so their stock prices are less sensitive to overall market swings — giving them low betas.","source":"Beta & Market Risk"},{"prompt":"A key limitation of beta is that:","options":["It simply cannot be calculated for individual stocks","It is backward-looking and may not predict future sensitivity","It completely ignores the dividend income a stock pays","It is really only relevant when analysing bonds"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Beta is estimated from historical data and is backward-looking. A company's sensitivity to the market can change significantly over time, making historical beta an imperfect predictor of future behaviour.","source":"Beta & Market Risk"}]},"dcf":{"title":"DCF","questions":[{"prompt":"What are the three sections of the cash flow statement?","options":["Revenue, total costs, and resulting profit","Operating, investing, and financing activities","Assets, liabilities, and shareholders' equity","Current, long-term, and total classifications"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Cash flow is split into operating (CFO), investing (CFI), and financing (CFF) activities.","source":"The Cash Flow Statement"},{"prompt":"Buying new equipment (capex) appears in which section?","options":["Operating","Financing","Investing","It does not appear"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Capital expenditure is an investing activity — cash used to acquire long-term assets.","source":"The Cash Flow Statement"},{"prompt":"Paying a dividend is classified as which type of cash flow?","options":["Operating","Investing","Financing","Non-cash"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Dividends paid are a financing outflow — cash returned to shareholders.","source":"The Cash Flow Statement"},{"prompt":"A common approximation of free cash flow is…","options":["Net income added on top of total revenue","Operating cash flow minus capital expenditure","Total revenue with COGS subtracted from it","Total assets minus the company's liabilities"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Free cash flow ≈ operating cash flow − capex: cash left after maintaining/growing the asset base.","source":"The Cash Flow Statement"},{"prompt":"Why can a profitable company still run out of cash?","options":["Net income uses accruals and non-cash items, so profit ≠ cash","Reported profit is essentially always a negative figure","Cash and accounting profit are one and the same thing","It is entirely down to the dividends that get paid out"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Accrual accounting and non-cash items mean reported profit can diverge from actual cash — hence the cash flow statement.","source":"The Cash Flow Statement"},{"prompt":"A company has EBIT of £60m, a 25% tax rate, and invested capital of £400m. What is its ROIC?","options":["15%","11.25%","10%","25%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"NOPAT = EBIT × (1 − tax rate) = £60m × (1 − 0.25) = £60m × 0.75 = £45m. ROIC = NOPAT / Invested Capital = £45m / £400m = 11.25%. Note: we use EBIT (not net income) because ROIC measures returns before financing — interest is not subtracted. The tax adjustment converts operating profit to an after-tax basis so ROIC and WACC are comparable on the same post-tax basis.","source":"ROIC and Economic Profit"},{"prompt":"Company X has ROIC = 20% and WACC = 12%. Company Y has ROIC = 8% and WACC = 12%. Both have £1bn of invested capital. What is the EVA for each?","options":["X: £200m created and Y: £80m created — both genuinely add economic value","X: +£80m, Y: −£40m","X: +£80m, Y: −£40m — X creates economic value; Y destroys it","X: £120m, Y: £96m"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"EVA = (ROIC − WACC) × Invested Capital. Company X: (20% − 12%) × £1bn = 8% × £1bn = +£80m of economic profit. Company Y: (8% − 12%) × £1bn = −4% × £1bn = −£40m of economic loss. Despite Y having positive reported profits (8% ROIC generates positive NOPAT), it is destroying value because its returns fall short of capital costs. Every pound Y reinvests reduces economic value. Y should return capital to shareholders rather than grow.","source":"ROIC and Economic Profit"},{"prompt":"A PE firm acquires a business for £300m. The business had pre-acquisition invested capital of £100m and pre-goodwill ROIC of 22%. After the acquisition, including £200m of goodwill, what is the post-goodwill ROIC if NOPAT is £22m?","options":["22%","7.3%","11%","15%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Post-goodwill invested capital = pre-acquisition capital + goodwill = £100m + £200m = £300m. Post-goodwill ROIC = NOPAT / total invested capital = £22m / £300m = 7.3%. The acquisition price was 3× book value. The underlying business is excellent (22% pre-goodwill ROIC) but the PE firm paid such a high premium that the blended return falls to 7.3% — potentially below WACC. This is the \"winner's curse\" in M&A: a great business acquired at too high a price becomes a poor investment.","source":"ROIC and Economic Profit"},{"prompt":"A company's ROIC has declined from 18% to 11% over five years while its WACC remains 9%. What is the most important strategic implication?","options":["The company should accelerate capital investment to recover ROIC through scale","ROIC remains above WACC so value is still being created — no action required","A falling ROIC nearing WACC signals eroding advantage — investigate the cause and consider returning capital","The company should reduce its WACC by taking on more debt to restore the ROIC/WACC spread"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"At 11%, ROIC is still above WACC (9%) — value is being created, but less efficiently than before. The trend is more alarming than the current level: declining ROIC typically signals intensifying competition, pricing pressure, or capital misallocation. If the trend continues and ROIC falls below WACC, the company becomes a value destroyer. Management must investigate the root cause — is it a structural threat to margins or a temporary issue? If the former, a capital return strategy (buybacks, dividends) may be better than continued reinvestment at declining returns.","source":"ROIC and Economic Profit"},{"prompt":"A stock with a beta of 1.5 would be expected to:","options":["Return 1.5% per year regardless of the market","Move 50% more than the market in each direction","Have a 50% chance of beating the market","Be 1.5× more diversified than a benchmark"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Beta of 1.5 means the stock is expected to amplify market moves by 50% — up and down. If the market rises 10%, the stock would be expected to rise 15%; if the market falls 10%, the stock would fall 15%.","source":"Beta & Market Risk"},{"prompt":"According to CAPM, investors should only be compensated for:","options":["Unsystematic (company-specific) risk","Systematic (market) risk","Both systematic and unsystematic risk","Volatility that exceeds 15% per year"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CAPM says unsystematic risk can be diversified away for free, so the market only rewards investors for bearing systematic (market) risk — captured by beta.","source":"Beta & Market Risk"},{"prompt":"If you hold 50% in a stock with beta 2.0 and 50% in a stock with beta 0, the portfolio beta is:","options":["0","1.0","1.5","2.0"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Portfolio beta = weighted average of component betas. (0.5 × 2.0) + (0.5 × 0) = 1.0. The portfolio moves in line with the market on average.","source":"Beta & Market Risk"},{"prompt":"Which type of stock typically has a low or negative beta?","options":["High-growth technology companies","Leveraged small-cap companies","Utilities and consumer staples","Emerging market equities"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Utilities and consumer staples sell things people buy regardless of the economic cycle (electricity, food). Their earnings are stable, so their stock prices are less sensitive to overall market swings — giving them low betas.","source":"Beta & Market Risk"},{"prompt":"A key limitation of beta is that:","options":["It simply cannot be calculated for individual stocks","It is backward-looking and may not predict future sensitivity","It completely ignores the dividend income a stock pays","It is really only relevant when analysing bonds"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Beta is estimated from historical data and is backward-looking. A company's sensitivity to the market can change significantly over time, making historical beta an imperfect predictor of future behaviour.","source":"Beta & Market Risk"},{"prompt":"In finance, risk is most commonly measured as:","options":["The chance of losing all your money","The standard deviation of returns","The maximum possible loss","The correlation with the market"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Standard deviation of returns — volatility — is the dominant risk measure in portfolio management. It captures the dispersion of returns around the average.","source":"Risk & Return"},{"prompt":"The equity risk premium refers to:","options":["The extra return equities must offer above the risk-free rate","The tax advantage of holding equities","The dividend yield on a diversified equity portfolio","The volatility of equity returns relative to bonds"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The equity risk premium is the extra expected return equities offer investors to compensate for their higher risk compared to holding a risk-free asset like a government bond. Historically around 4–6% per year.","source":"Risk & Return"},{"prompt":"A portfolio with an expected return of 8% and standard deviation of 12% should have returns within the range of -4% to +20% approximately:","options":["50% of the time","68% of the time","95% of the time","99% of the time"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"One standard deviation above and below the mean captures approximately 68% of outcomes in a normal distribution. So -4% to +20% (8% ± 12%) should occur about two-thirds of the time.","source":"Risk & Return"},{"prompt":"Why can long-horizon investors generally afford to take more risk?","options":["They are simply smarter at picking individual stocks","They have time to wait out volatility and recover from drawdowns","They pay a noticeably lower rate of tax on their gains","They have privileged access to far better information"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Long time horizons allow investors to ride out market downturns without being forced to sell at a loss. The longer your horizon, the more volatility you can absorb — historically, equity markets have always recovered and gone on to new highs given enough time.","source":"Risk & Return"},{"prompt":"The difference between expected return and realised return is:","options":["The compensation known as the equity risk premium","It is essentially always zero over the long run","The result of actual market outcomes deviating from forecasts","Exactly the same thing as the standard deviation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Expected return is the probability-weighted average outcome. Realised return is what actually happened. In any given period, actual outcomes can differ significantly from expectations due to randomness and unforeseen events.","source":"Risk & Return"}]},"lbo":{"title":"Private equity & LBO","questions":[{"prompt":"A PE firm raises a £500m fund. The management fee is 2% on committed capital. How much does the GP earn annually in management fees?","options":["£2m","£10m","£100m","£25m"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"2% × £500m = £10m per year. This is the GP's predictable operating income regardless of fund performance — it covers salaries, rent, travel, and due diligence costs. A typical mid-market PE fund with £500m might have 20-30 investment professionals. £10m per year supports a modest team but is not the main economic upside — that comes from the 20% carried interest on successful exits.","source":"The PE Business Model"},{"prompt":"A PE fund invests £1bn and exits with £2.2bn. The hurdle rate is 8% per annum over 5 years. What is the approximate profit subject to carried interest?","options":["£1.2bn — the total profit","Approximately £733m — profit above the 8% hurdle on £1bn over 5 years","£200m — the carry is 20% of total profit automatically","Zero — the hurdle requires 8× return, not 8% annual"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The hurdle return: £1bn × (1.08)^5 = £1.469bn — LPs must receive this before carry kicks in. Profit above the hurdle: £2.2bn − £1.469bn = £731m. GP carry at 20%: £731m × 20% = £146m. LPs get: £1.469bn + £731m × 80% = £2.054bn. The GP's £146m carry vastly exceeds the £50m management fees (£10m/yr × 5yr) — carry is the primary GP incentive, aligning them with LP returns.","source":"The PE Business Model"},{"prompt":"Under the European waterfall, when does the GP first receive carried interest?","options":["After each portfolio company is exited, on a deal-by-deal basis as the sale proceeds arrive","Only after all LPs get their capital back plus the 8% preferred return across the whole fund","Immediately at fund closing, as an upfront carry payment","After five years, regardless of performance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The European waterfall (also called \"whole fund\" waterfall) requires all LP committed capital to be returned and the preferred return (hurdle) achieved across the entire fund before any carry is distributed. This protects LPs from a scenario where early exits are profitable but later ones lose money — under the American deal-by-deal waterfall, the GP could collect carry on winning deals even if the fund as a whole underperforms. Most European and Asian PE funds use the European waterfall.","source":"The PE Business Model"},{"prompt":"What is the \"J-curve\" in private equity?","options":["The typical overall shape of LBO returns — a sharp initial gain followed by a gradual decline","The early period of negative returns from fees, before portfolio exits generate positive cash flows","A graph of leverage versus return — showing diminishing returns as debt increases","The distribution of returns across a portfolio — J-shaped means a few winners drive all performance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The J-curve describes the timing pattern of PE fund cash flows and returns: in years 1-3, management fees and initial investments create negative cash flows / modest valuations (the bottom of the J). As portfolio companies are grown and eventually exited in years 5-10, returns spike significantly (the upswing of the J). LPs must accept initial negative returns before realising the upside — which is why vintage year diversification is important. The J-curve effect is more pronounced for first-time funds building a portfolio from scratch.","source":"The PE Business Model"},{"prompt":"A PE firm acquires a company for £400m (8× EV/EBITDA on £50m EBITDA) using 30% equity (£120m) and 70% debt (£280m). After 5 years, EBITDA grows to £70m and the company is sold at 9× EV/EBITDA. Ignoring debt paydown, what is the exit equity value and approximate MOIC?","options":["Exit equity = £630m; MOIC = 5.25×","Exit equity = £350m; MOIC = 2.9×","Exit equity = £170m; MOIC = 1.4×","Exit equity = £280m; MOIC = 2.3×"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Exit EV = 9 × £70m EBITDA = £630m. Subtract remaining debt (£280m, assumed unchanged for simplicity): equity = £630m − £280m = £350m. MOIC = £350m / £120m = 2.92×. This illustrates two drivers: EBITDA growth (£50m → £70m = +40%) and multiple expansion (8× → 9×). In practice, debt also amortises over 5 years (reducing faster with cash generation), which would increase equity value further.","source":"The LBO Model"},{"prompt":"Why do PE firms use high leverage in LBOs rather than funding acquisitions entirely with equity?","options":["Debt is always cheaper than equity, so funding a deal with 100% debt would maximise the returns","Leverage amplifies equity returns — enterprise-value gains are magnified on the small equity base","PE firms do not have enough capital to fund acquisitions with equity alone","Debt financing provides tax benefits that make the acquisition cheaper"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Leverage is the core mechanism of LBO returns. Example: a company is worth £100m; the PE firm invests £40m equity + £60m debt. If the company value rises to £130m and debt is unchanged, equity value = £70m — a 75% return on the £40m invested. An unlevered investor who bought a 40% equity stake for £40m (no debt) would hold a stake worth 40% × £130m = £52m at exit — just 30% return. The debt lever transformed the same £40m into a 75% return by concentrating the full upside of enterprise value growth onto the smaller equity base.","source":"The LBO Model"},{"prompt":"A PE fund returns 3× MOIC on a 5-year hold. A second fund returns 3× MOIC on a 3-year hold. Which performs better on an IRR basis?","options":["Equal — MOIC is the same so IRR must be the same","The 3-year fund — same MOIC achieved faster generates a higher IRR","The 5-year fund — longer holds generate more compounding","IRR cannot be compared without knowing the fund size"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"IRR accounts for the time value of money. The same MOIC achieved faster generates a higher IRR because you get your money back sooner and can redeploy it. 3× in 3 years ≈ 44% IRR (£1 → £3 over 3 years). 3× in 5 years ≈ 25% IRR (£1 → £3 over 5 years). For LP investors, faster return of capital is better — they can redeploy into new vintage years. This is why PE firms sometimes use \"continuation vehicles\" or dividend recaps to accelerate distributions even without a full exit.","source":"The LBO Model"},{"prompt":"Which company characteristic makes it a particularly attractive LBO target?","options":["High capital expenditure requirements — the company needs continuous reinvestment","Highly cyclical revenues — the volatility creates buying opportunities","Stable, predictable, and growing free cash flow with low capital intensity","A recent large acquisition that has yet to be integrated"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The ideal LBO target has: (1) stable, predictable cash flows to service the debt (LBOs fail when companies cannot make debt payments); (2) low capex requirements — cash after maintenance capex goes to debt repayment; (3) a proven, profitable business model; (4) opportunities for operational improvement or bolt-on acquisitions. High capex (like semiconductors or steel) leaves little cash for debt service. Cyclical revenues are risky — a recession could prevent debt payment. Serial acquirers with undigested deals add integration risk.","source":"The LBO Model"},{"prompt":"A PE firm improves the pricing of its portfolio company by 2%. Revenue was £200m with 40% EBITDA margins. If the company is exited at 10× EV/EBITDA, how much enterprise value does this pricing improvement create?","options":["£4m","£40m","£16m","£80m"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Pricing improvement of 2% on £200m revenue = £4m additional revenue. At 40% margins, incremental revenue flows through at high contribution margin — assume most drops to EBITDA: £4m × ~100% (pricing is pure margin once fixed costs are covered) = £4m additional EBITDA. At 10× EV/EBITDA multiple: £4m × 10 = £40m of enterprise value created. This is why pricing is the highest-leverage value creation lever — it flows directly to margin with no incremental cost.","source":"Value Creation"},{"prompt":"Why do PE-backed companies typically have more intensive financial oversight than public companies?","options":["PE regulations require monthly filings with the SEC, versus only quarterly for public companies","PE firms have board control and shift to monthly reporting, with hands-on GP involvement in strategy","Public companies are too large to manage closely — PE companies are always smaller","PE-backed companies must report to their banks monthly as a loan covenant"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"PE firms hold board control (typically 3-5 board seats in a control buyout) and are actively incentivised to maximise returns on a 5-7 year timeline. They move financial reporting to monthly cadence, establish detailed KPIs, and are directly involved in strategic hiring and capital allocation decisions. Public companies are accountable to dispersed shareholders and typically operate on quarterly reporting with less intensive governance. This \"GP governance\" is seen as a key value creation advantage of the PE ownership model.","source":"Value Creation"},{"prompt":"A PE firm acquires a plumbing services company (the \"platform\") at 8× EBITDA on £5m EBITDA. It then acquires three small plumbing businesses at 5× EBITDA, each with £2m EBITDA (total: £6m additional EBITDA for £30m). It exits the combined business (£11m EBITDA) at 10× EBITDA. What is the multiple arbitrage gain?","options":["£0 — there is no multiple arbitrage if the exit multiple equals the platform entry multiple","£30m — the bolt-ons were bought at 5× but exit at 10×, creating £5 per £1 of bolt-on EBITDA","The bolt-ons create £60m of EV at exit vs £30m cost — £30m gained","The platform entry multiple (8×) is already below exit (10×), so there is no bolt-on arbitrage"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The bolt-on EBITDA (£6m) is acquired at 5× = £30m total cost. At exit, this EBITDA is valued at 10× (same as the rest of the platform) = £60m of enterprise value. The arbitrage gain = £60m − £30m = £30m. This is the \"multiple re-rating\" of smaller acquisitions when combined with the larger platform and exited as part of a market leader. The PE firm effectively bought earnings at 5× and sells them at 10× — a common source of LBO returns in buy-and-build strategies.","source":"Value Creation"},{"prompt":"A portfolio company improves its EBITDA margin from 20% to 25% on flat revenue of £100m. At exit with a 10× EV/EBITDA multiple, how much enterprise value does this margin improvement create?","options":["£5m","£50m","£25m","£100m"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"EBITDA improvement: 25% − 20% = 5% margin on £100m revenue = £5m additional EBITDA. At 10× EV/EBITDA: £5m × 10 = £50m of additional enterprise value. This is the power of margin improvement in PE — every pound of EBITDA created is multiplied by the exit multiple. Combined with leverage (which amplifies the equity return on that EV increase), this demonstrates why operational improvement has such a magnified effect on PE equity returns.","source":"Value Creation"},{"prompt":"Why do strategic acquirers typically pay higher prices in PE exit auctions than other PE firms (financial buyers)?","options":["Strategic buyers have more access to debt financing","Strategic buyers can pay a synergy premium — they value the combined business above standalone value","Financial buyers are constrained by carried interest calculations that cap bids","Strategic buyers do not need to model returns — they have unlimited capital"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Strategic buyers can pay above standalone value because they expect synergies: cost savings (eliminating duplicate functions), revenue synergies (cross-selling, geographic expansion), or technology acquisition. A software company acquiring a competitor might save £20m/year in costs + earn £10m in cross-sell revenue — justifying a significantly higher price than a financial buyer who can only model standalone performance plus financial engineering. This synergy premium is why PE firms often run dual-track processes to keep strategic bidders competing with financial ones.","source":"Exit Strategies"},{"prompt":"A PE firm sells its portfolio company to another PE firm (a secondary buyout). What is a common criticism of SBOs?","options":["SBOs are illegal in most jurisdictions due to conflicts of interest","They can be \"passing the parcel\" — adding leverage without genuine operational value creation","Financial buyers always pay less than strategic buyers, reducing LP returns","SBOs require the selling PE firm to reinvest proceeds in the same industry"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"SBO critics argue that when a company is sold from one PE firm to another, the new buyer often simply adds more debt (re-leveraging) and applies similar operational playbooks without genuine strategic transformation. The third or fourth buyout of the same business, each time with more leverage, increases default risk without creating proportionate economic value. However, good SBOs add value through fresh management incentives, new capital for bolt-ons, or accessing new markets that the previous PE owner was unable to execute.","source":"Exit Strategies"},{"prompt":"A PE firm wants to return capital to LPs while retaining ownership of a growing portfolio company. Which route achieves this?","options":["A trade sale, meaning you sell the whole company outright to a strategic corporate buyer","Secondary buyout — sell to another PE firm","Dividend recapitalisation — take on additional debt to pay a special dividend","IPO — list on the public market and sell shares"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A dividend recapitalisation allows the PE firm to retain ownership while returning capital to LPs. The portfolio company takes on additional debt (e.g. a new term loan) and pays the proceeds as a special dividend to the equity holders (the PE fund). This increases LP IRR (faster capital return) without surrendering the asset — useful when the PE firm believes there is still significant upside remaining but LPs want capital returned. The risk is increased leverage on the company.","source":"Exit Strategies"},{"prompt":"Why is an IPO typically used for only the highest-quality PE portfolio companies?","options":["IPOs are cheaper to run than an M&A sale, so only premium companies can justify the legal costs","Public markets apply higher multiples to smaller companies","IPOs take 12–18 months, need public disclosure, and face volatile pricing — only strong leaders qualify","PE firms are prohibited from taking below-average companies public by SEC rules"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"IPOs are complex, time-consuming (12-18 months of preparation), and costly (regulatory compliance, investor relations, quarterly reporting). Post-IPO, the PE firm faces a 180-day lock-up and ongoing public scrutiny. Public market valuations can disconnect from fundamentals and are volatile. Only companies with compelling growth stories, clean corporate governance, and the scale to attract institutional investor demand make good IPO candidates. Weaker or more cyclical businesses are better candidates for strategic sale or SBO.","source":"Exit Strategies"},{"prompt":"A PE fund has invested £500m and distributed £600m to LPs so far. It still holds portfolio companies valued at £400m (unrealised NAV). What are the DPI and TVPI?","options":["DPI = 2.0×; TVPI = 2.0×","DPI = 1.2×; TVPI = 2.0×","DPI = 0.6×; TVPI = 1.0×","DPI = 1.2×; TVPI = 1.4×"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"DPI = Distributions / Paid-In = £600m / £500m = 1.2×. LPs have received 1.2× their invested capital back in cash. TVPI = (Distributions + Unrealised NAV) / Paid-In = (£600m + £400m) / £500m = 2.0×. The gap between TVPI (2.0×) and DPI (1.2×) = 0.8× of unrealised value — this value exists on paper but has not been converted to cash. LPs care about both: DPI shows realised returns; TVPI shows total potential if unrealised marks prove accurate.","source":"PE Performance Metrics"},{"prompt":"A PE fund's gross IRR is 28% and net IRR is 18%. The management fee is 2% and carry is 20%. What explains the ~10pp gap between gross and net?","options":["The entire 10-point difference comes purely from the 20% carried interest charged on 28% returns","Management fees compounding over the fund life plus 20% carried interest cut the gross return to LPs","The fund is currently in the J-curve phase — gross and net will converge at exit","Net IRR includes public market index performance subtracted from gross"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The gap reflects both fee components: (1) Management fees: 2%/year on £500m over 10 years = £100m; this reduces LP capital available to invest. (2) Carried interest: 20% of profits above the 8% hurdle — on strong returns, carry is the dominant drag. Together, a fund generating 28% gross can net 18-20% to LPs. This is why LPs scrutinise gross-to-net spread carefully — high fees from smaller managers or multiple layers of funds (fund of funds) can erode returns to below public market levels.","source":"PE Performance Metrics"},{"prompt":"What is a PME (Public Market Equivalent) analysis used for in PE performance evaluation?","options":["Calculating the maximum amount of debt a target company can support in a leveraged buyout deal","Comparing PE fund returns to what would have been earned in a public market index with the same timing of cash flows","Estimating the fair value of unrealised portfolio companies","Measuring the impact of management fees across different fund structures"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"PME answers the fundamental LP question: \"Was PE worth it versus just investing in an index fund?\" It does this by simulating what would have happened if the same capital calls had been invested in a public index (e.g. S&P 500) and the same distributions had been taken out. If the PE fund's actual IRR exceeds the PME IRR, it genuinely outperformed public markets on a like-for-like timing basis. PME is now standard in LP reporting — sophisticated LPs (endowments, pension funds) use it to assess every manager they consider.","source":"PE Performance Metrics"},{"prompt":"Research shows that PE manager performance \"persists\" — top-quartile managers tend to stay top-quartile. What are the implications for LP investors?","options":["LPs should rotate out of performing managers and into laggard managers — mean reversion will benefit them","Past performance in PE is a meaningful signal — LPs should prioritise access to managers with strong track records","The best PE returns come from the most recent vintage years regardless of manager quality","Manager persistence is a myth — PE returns are entirely driven by market timing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Unlike in public equity mutual funds (where past performance does NOT predict future performance), PE fund manager skill tends to persist across fund generations. Research by Kaplan & Schoar shows that a manager in the top quartile of one fund is likely to be top quartile in the next. This means track record is meaningful for LP due diligence — unlike public market manager selection. It also explains why the best PE managers (Blackstone, KKR, Sequoia for VC) can raise oversubscribed funds: LPs rationally pay a premium for access to proven managers.","source":"PE Performance Metrics"}]},"markets":{"title":"Markets & macro","questions":[{"prompt":"What does duration measure?","options":["The time remaining until the first coupon is paid","A bond’s price sensitivity to interest rate changes","The credit risk that the issuer might default","The size of the coupon the bond pays each year"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Duration estimates how much a bond’s price moves for a given change in rates (and is a weighted-average time to receive cash flows).","source":"Duration and Interest Rate Risk"},{"prompt":"Which has higher duration, a 2-year or a 10-year bond (all else equal)?","options":["The 2-year","They are equal","The 10-year","Neither has duration"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Longer maturity means higher duration and greater price sensitivity to rate changes.","source":"Duration and Interest Rate Risk"},{"prompt":"What is modified duration used for?","options":["Estimating the % price change for a 1% change in yield","Calculating the size of the next coupon payment","Finding the exact date the bond finally matures","Assigning a credit rating to the bond's issuer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Modified duration approximates the % price change for a 100bp move in yield (duration 5 ≈ −5% for +1%).","source":"Duration and Interest Rate Risk"},{"prompt":"Why do pension funds focus heavily on duration?","options":["Purely in order to maximise the total coupon income the investor receives over the years","To avoid ever having to hold bonds at all","To increase the investor's corporate voting rights","To match the rate sensitivity of assets to their long-dated liabilities"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Pension liabilities are long-dated and rate-sensitive; matching asset and liability duration immunises funding against rate moves.","source":"Duration and Interest Rate Risk"},{"prompt":"What is convexity?","options":["The curvature in the price/yield relationship that duration alone misses","The frequency with which the coupon is paid out","The probability that the issuer eventually defaults","The size of the bid/ask spread that dealers quote on the bond in the open secondary market"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Convexity captures how duration itself changes as yields move, refining the price estimate for larger rate moves.","source":"Duration and Interest Rate Risk"},{"prompt":"What does a normal yield curve look like?","options":["Perfectly flat across every maturity on the curve","Downward sloping, with short maturities yielding more","Upward sloping — longer maturities yield more","Vertical, with all maturities at a single yield"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A normal curve slopes upward: investors demand more yield to lend for longer, consistent with growth expectations.","source":"The Yield Curve"},{"prompt":"What does an inverted yield curve historically signal?","options":["Strong imminent growth","A possible recession ahead","Zero inflation","A market holiday"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Inversion (short > long yields) has historically preceded recessions, reflecting expectations of future rate cuts/slowdown.","source":"The Yield Curve"},{"prompt":"What is yield curve \"steepening\"?","options":["The gap between long and short yields widens","The curve flattens toward a single common yield","All yields across the curve fall by equal amounts","Short-dated yields rise above long-dated yields"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Steepening means long-term yields rise relative to short-term (or short-term fall faster), widening the spread.","source":"The Yield Curve"},{"prompt":"What is the \"2s10s\" spread?","options":["The 2-year yield minus the 30-year bond yield","The spread between two bonds' coupon rates","The bid/ask spread quoted on the 10-year bond","The 10-year yield minus the 2-year yield"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"\"2s10s\" is the 10-year minus 2-year government yield — a closely watched gauge; negative values indicate inversion.","source":"The Yield Curve"},{"prompt":"The US yield curve inverted before the 2008 financial crisis.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — the curve (e.g. 2s10s) inverted in 2006–07, ahead of the 2008 recession, consistent with its historical record.","source":"The Yield Curve"},{"prompt":"What is most central banks’ primary objective?","options":["Maximising the government's total tax revenue","Price stability (low, stable inflation)","Actively pushing the domestic stock market higher","Setting the country's headline income tax rates"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Price stability — keeping inflation low and steady — is the core mandate of most central banks.","source":"What Central Banks Do"},{"prompt":"The US Federal Reserve’s \"dual mandate\" is…","options":["Growth and exports","Stable prices and maximum employment","Low taxes and high wages","Strong dollar and trade surplus"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Fed’s dual mandate is stable prices and maximum (full) employment.","source":"What Central Banks Do"},{"prompt":"Raising the policy interest rate is intended to…","options":["Stimulate borrowing","Cool demand and inflation","Increase the money supply","Weaken the banking system"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Higher rates raise borrowing costs, cooling demand and inflation; cuts do the reverse.","source":"What Central Banks Do"},{"prompt":"What does \"lender of last resort\" mean?","options":["Providing emergency liquidity to solvent banks in a crisis","Lending money directly to ordinary consumers","Buying company shares to prop up the equity market","Setting the government's overall tax policy"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In crises the central bank supplies emergency liquidity to stop panics and bank runs spreading.","source":"What Central Banks Do"},{"prompt":"Central bank independence helps anchor inflation expectations.","options":["True","False"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"True — credible independence lets banks make tough calls and keeps expectations anchored, easing inflation control.","source":"What Central Banks Do"},{"prompt":"What are open market operations?","options":["Buying and selling government securities to steer reserves and rates","Setting the national rate of income tax","Commissioning and issuing new currency designs","Directly regulating and supervising the national stock exchange and its members"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"OMOs add or drain bank reserves by buying/selling securities, keeping market rates near target.","source":"Monetary Policy Tools"},{"prompt":"Lowering reserve requirements tends to…","options":["Restrict bank lending","Free banks to lend more","Raise the policy rate","Reduce the money supply"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Lower reserve requirements let banks hold less against deposits, enabling more lending.","source":"Monetary Policy Tools"},{"prompt":"What is forward guidance?","options":["Communication about the likely future path of policy to shape expectations","Imposing a mandatory minimum reserve requirement on all commercial banks in the system","Granting a temporary tax break to households","A newly issued type of government bond"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Forward guidance signals future policy, influencing longer-term rates today via expectations.","source":"Monetary Policy Tools"},{"prompt":"When a central bank buys government securities in OMOs, it…","options":["Drains money from the system","Injects money (eases policy)","Raises taxes","Cancels the debt"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Buying securities injects reserves/money, easing policy; selling drains money, tightening.","source":"Monetary Policy Tools"},{"prompt":"What is the central bank’s main tool in normal times?","options":["The policy interest rate","The exchange rate peg","The income tax rate","Stock buybacks"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The policy (base) rate is the primary conventional tool, anchoring short-term rates economy-wide.","source":"Monetary Policy Tools"},{"prompt":"What does GDP measure?","options":["The total value of final goods and services produced","The size of the government's annual budget","The overall market value of the stock market","The economy's headline unemployment rate"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"GDP is the total value of all final goods and services produced over a period — the size of the economy.","source":"GDP and Growth"},{"prompt":"What is the difference between real and nominal GDP?","options":["Real GDP strips out inflation; nominal is at current prices","Nominal GDP is the measure that removes inflation","Real and nominal GDP are one and the same thing","Real GDP entirely ignores the economy's output"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Real GDP adjusts for inflation to show true output change; nominal GDP is at current prices.","source":"GDP and Growth"},{"prompt":"In most developed economies, the largest component of GDP is…","options":["Government spending","Consumer spending","Net exports","Investment"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Consumption (consumer spending) is typically the biggest single component of GDP.","source":"GDP and Growth"},{"prompt":"A common rule-of-thumb definition of a recession is…","options":["Two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP","A single month of falling equity-market prices","Any rise at all in the headline inflation rate","One unexpectedly weak monthly jobs report"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth is the widely used technical definition.","source":"GDP and Growth"},{"prompt":"When economists refer to \"growth,\" they usually mean…","options":["Nominal GDP growth","Real GDP growth","Stock market growth","Population growth"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Growth refers to real GDP growth — the inflation-adjusted change in output.","source":"GDP and Growth"},{"prompt":"What does CPI measure?","options":["The average change in prices consumers pay for a basket","The economy's headline unemployment rate, measured across the whole labour force","The rate at which real GDP is growing","The currency's prevailing exchange rate"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Consumer Price Index tracks the average change in consumer prices for a fixed basket.","source":"Inflation Measures"},{"prompt":"How does core inflation differ from headline?","options":["Core CPI excludes volatile food and energy prices","Core CPI includes only food and energy prices","Core and headline CPI are exactly identical","Core CPI excludes every category of goods"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Core strips out volatile food and energy to reveal the underlying inflation trend.","source":"Inflation Measures"},{"prompt":"Which inflation measure does the US Federal Reserve prefer?","options":["CPI","PCE","RPI","GDP deflator only"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Fed targets the PCE price index, which is broader and adjusts for substitution.","source":"Inflation Measures"},{"prompt":"What does inflation do to the purchasing power of money?","options":["Increases it","Erodes it","Has no effect","Fixes it permanently"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Rising prices mean each unit of money buys less — inflation erodes purchasing power.","source":"Inflation Measures"},{"prompt":"Compared with CPI, the UK’s RPI tends to…","options":["Run higher","Run lower","Be identical","Be negative"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"RPI uses a different formula and tends to run higher than CPI, though it’s been officially downgraded.","source":"Inflation Measures"},{"prompt":"The US 2-year yield rises to 5.3% while the 10-year stays at 4.1%. What is the 2s10s spread, and what does it signal?","options":["+1.2% (120bps) — a normal, healthy upward-sloping curve","−1.2% (−120bps) — an inverted curve, historically a reliable recession predictor","+9.4% — the sum of both yields, signalling strong growth","Exactly 0% — the yield curve is considered flat whenever both of the yields are above 4%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"2s10s spread = 10yr − 2yr = 4.1% − 5.3% = −1.2% (−120bps). A negative spread means the curve is inverted — short rates are above long rates. The bond market is pricing in future rate cuts (expected recession → Fed eases). This specific inversion (2yr > 10yr) preceded every US recession since 1970, with a lead time of roughly 12-18 months. The depth of inversion also matters — −120bps is a significantly deep inversion, historically associated with more severe recessions.","source":"The Yield Curve"},{"prompt":"According to the Pure Expectations Theory, if the current 1-year rate is 4% and the expected 1-year rate next year is 6%, what is the fair 2-year rate today?","options":["6%","4%","5%","10%"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under Pure Expectations Theory, the 2-year rate = geometric average of expected future 1-year rates: (1 + r₂)² = (1.04) × (1.06), so r₂ = √(1.04 × 1.06) − 1 = √1.1024 − 1 ≈ 5.0%. Approximately: (4% + 6%) / 2 = 5%. Intuitively, investing for 2 years should give the same return as investing for 1 year at 4% and rolling over into another year at 6% — otherwise arbitrage would push rates into alignment.","source":"The Yield Curve"},{"prompt":"A macro trader believes the Federal Reserve will begin cutting rates aggressively over the next 12 months. Which yield curve trade best expresses this view?","options":["A bear flattener trade: go short the 30-year bonds while going long the 2-year bonds","Bull steepener: long 2-year bonds, short 30-year bonds","Bull steepener: long 2-year bonds, which rally as rates are cut, and short or neutral the 30-year","Curve flattener: short 2-year bonds, long 30-year bonds"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When the Fed cuts rates, short-end yields fall fastest and furthest — 2-year yields are most directly tied to near-term policy rate expectations. A bull steepener (rates fall, curve steepens) is the classic Fed-cutting trade: long the front end (2yr bonds rally significantly), neutral or short the long end (30yr is driven more by inflation and term premium, which may not fall as much or may rise if rate cuts reflate the economy). This is called a \"bull steepener\" because rates are falling (bond bull market) and the curve is steepening (front end rallying more than the long end).","source":"The Yield Curve"},{"prompt":"What is \"roll-down return\" in the context of fixed income?","options":["The return earned from rolling maturing bonds into brand-new, longer-dated bonds each period","The gain from a bond rolling to shorter maturities down an upward-sloping curve, into lower yields","The return generated by rotating from short-duration to long-duration bonds","The loss incurred when a bond's coupon is below the risk-free rate"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Roll-down return exploits the normal upward-sloping yield curve. A 10-year bond bought at 4.5% yield \"rolls\" to become a 9-year bond after one year. If the curve is unchanged, it is now priced at the 9-year yield (say 4.3%). The fall in yield means the price rises — generating a return beyond the coupon. In a steep, stable curve, roll-down can add 0.5-1.0% annually to total return. Active bond managers target high roll-down points on the curve (where the slope is steepest) to maximise this effect.","source":"The Yield Curve"}]}};
